IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `art-dates`

-- Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) -- baseline encoding from the seat of the applicant: the person whose
-- interests are affected by a Commonwealth decision (a refused or reduced compensation claim, a social security or
-- veterans' decision, a Commonwealth workers' compensation determination, or any other decision an Act or instrument makes
-- reviewable) and who asks for reasons, applies to the Administrative Review Tribunal, takes part in the review, and goes
-- on to the guidance and appeals panel, second review or the Federal Court.
--
-- SEAT. The applicant (and, in s 84, the person who steps in when the applicant dies or becomes bankrupt). Questions are
-- phrased as that person asks them. The Tribunal, the President, the decision-maker, the Attorney-General and the Federal
-- Court get no seat: their satisfactions, opinions, orders and determinations are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The three duties with a deadline (s 18 my application; s 269 the decision-maker's answer to my request for
-- reasons; ss 23 and 27 the decision-maker's reasons and documents) are regulative rules run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: ss 12, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 23, 27, 28(3)-(9), 29, 31, 32, 66, 84, 95-102, 107, 108(3)-(5), 111,
-- 112(5)-(7), 123, 125, 131C, 131D, 131H, 131J, 131W, 131X, 172-174, 266, 268-271, 294. What is out, and why, is in
-- NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024, No. 40, 2024, Compilation No. 5, compilation date 18 May 2026
-- (register id C2026C00202), deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2024A00040.txt. The Administrative Review
-- Tribunal Rules and the practice directions that set the applicant's numbers (the period to apply, s 18(1); fees, s 296;
-- the time to give the Tribunal's decision, s 111(3)) are not deposited, so each is an input.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Tribunal or another official are
-- INPUTS ("if the Tribunal considers that ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): JUST FALSE is the text's own no; NOTHING is where the text is silent or limited by its own wording.

§ `Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 -- the applicant`

DECLARE `Applicant party` IS ONE OF `I, the applicant`, `the decision-maker`
DECLARE `Review step` IS ONE OF
    `apply to the Tribunal for review`
    `answer my request for reasons (a statement of reasons or a written refusal)`
    `give me the statement of reasons and the relevant documents`

-- s 15: an organisation or association "is taken to be a person whose interests are affected by a decision if: (a) the
-- decision relates to a matter included in the objects or purposes of the organisation or association at the time the
-- decision is made; and (b) the matter has not been removed from the objects or purposes". "Interests ... affected" is
-- otherwise not defined: asked as a fact.
GIVEN `affected as a fact` IS A BOOLEAN, `an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN, `in its objects then` IS A BOOLEAN, `since removed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 15, 17 -- my interests are affected` `affected as a fact` `an organisation` `in its objects then` `since removed` IF
        `affected as a fact`
    OR (`an organisation` AND `in its objects then` AND (NOT `since removed`))

-- s 12(1): "A decision is a reviewable decision if an Act or a legislative instrument provides for an application to be
-- made to the Tribunal for review of the decision." (2): also a decision under a power whose decisions are made reviewable,
-- "whether or not it is made by the person". Whether the enabling law provides for review is asked as a fact.
GIVEN `review of this decision` IS A BOOLEAN, `review under this power` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 12 -- a reviewable decision` `review of this decision` `review under this power` IF
    `review of this decision` OR `review under this power`

§§ `Notice of the decision and reasons from the decision-maker (Part 10)`

-- s 266(1): applies "If any decision in a review pathway is or would be a reviewable decision". (2): "does not apply in
-- relation to a decision by a court or a decision by the Tribunal". (4): "The decision-maker must take reasonable steps to
-- give notice of the decision to any person whose interests are affected". (5): the notice "must include notice of the
-- right (if any) to have the decision reviewed, including ... the right to apply to the Tribunal". (6): "does not
-- require the decision-maker to give notice if: (a) another Act or an instrument ... requires notice to be given of review
-- rights ...; or (b) the decision is taken to have been made by ... section 16 ... or section 10 of the Ombudsman Act 1976;
-- or (c) the decision is [not to impose a liability; a class-wide adjustment of periodic payments; the most favourable
-- category of entitlement] ... and does not adversely affect the interests of any other person." (7): failure "does not
-- affect the validity of the decision."
-- JUST TRUE: the decision-maker must take reasonable steps to tell me, with my review rights. JUST FALSE: s 266(6) (the
-- text's no: "does not require"). NOTHING: outside a review pathway, a court's or the Tribunal's decision (s 266(1), (2);
-- fork F1), or my interests are not affected (the text is silent).
@export Must the decision-maker tell me about a decision that affects me, and about my right to have it reviewed by the Administrative Review Tribunal (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 266)? The decision-maker must take reasonable steps to give notice of the decision, including the right to apply to the Tribunal, unless another law sets the review-rights notice, the decision was deemed because time ran out, or the decision is wholly favourable and harms no one else. A failure does not make the decision invalid.
GIVEN `in a review pathway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this decision, or a decision on review or further review of it, is (or would be) one an Act or legislative instrument lets you take to the Administrative Review Tribunal
      `by a court or the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made by a court, or by the Tribunal itself
      `my interests are affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision affects your interests
      `another law sets the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act or instrument already requires notice of review rights to be given for this decision
      `deemed because time ran out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is one taken to have been made because the decision-maker did not act in time (s 16 of this Act, or s 10 of the Ombudsman Act 1976)
      `a favourable kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is one not to impose a liability, penalty or limitation on you, a payment adjustment made alike for a whole class, or one putting you in the most favourable category of a monetary entitlement
      `adverse to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision adversely affects anyone else's interests
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the decision-maker tell me of the decision and my review rights question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `in a review pathway`) OR `by a court or the Tribunal` OR (NOT `my interests are affected`)
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `another law sets the notice` OR `deemed because time ran out` OR (`a favourable kind` AND (NOT `adverse to someone else`))
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 268(1): "A person whose interests are affected by a reviewable decision may request the decision-maker to give the
-- person a statement of reasons for the decision." (2): "in writing". s 269(6): "the decision-maker must refuse the request
-- if the person's interests are not affected by the decision."
-- JUST TRUE: I may ask, in writing. JUST FALSE: my interests are not affected (s 269(6), the text's no). NOTHING: the
-- decision is not reviewable (Part 10 Division 3 does not reach it).
@export Can I ask the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons for a decision that I could take to the Administrative Review Tribunal (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 15, 268)? A person whose interests are affected by a reviewable decision may ask, in writing. An organisation is affected if the decision concerns a matter in its objects when the decision was made and the matter has not since been removed.
GIVEN `review of this decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or legislative instrument provides for applying to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of this decision
      `review under this power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or instrument provides for Tribunal review of decisions made under the power used (even if the person who made this decision was not authorised)
      `affected as a fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision affects your interests
      `an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an organisation or association (incorporated or not) rather than an individual
      `in its objects then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are an organisation: whether the decision relates to a matter in your objects or purposes at the time it was made
      `since removed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are an organisation: whether that matter has since been removed from your objects or purposes
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask for a statement of reasons question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`s 12 -- a reviewable decision` `review of this decision` `review under this power`)
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `ss 15, 17 -- my interests are affected` `affected as a fact` `an organisation` `in its objects then` `since removed`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 269(2): "Within 28 days after receiving the request, the decision-maker must give the person a statement of reasons".
-- (6) must refuse if not affected; (7) "may refuse the request if the person has already been given a statement of
-- reasons"; (8) may refuse a late request: "(a) if the decision was set out in a document given to the person ... if the
-- request is not made within 28 days after the document was given to the person; and (b) if [not] ... within a reasonable
-- time after the decision is made"; (9) may refuse if the statement "would disclose information that concerns another
-- person" and removal "is not reasonably practicable"; (10) may refuse under a s 272 certificate if "the statement would be
-- false or misleading if the information were removed". (11), (12): a refusal (other than under (7)) needs written notice
-- with the reason, "within 28 days after receiving the request".
DECLARE `What happens to my request for reasons` IS ONE OF
    `the decision-maker must refuse: my interests are not affected (s 269(6)); written notice of refusal within 28 days (s 269(11), (12))`
    `the decision-maker may refuse: I have already been given a statement of reasons (s 269(7)); no refusal notice is needed`
    `the decision-maker may refuse: my request was late (s 269(8)); a refusal needs written notice within 28 days (s 269(11), (12))`
    `the decision-maker may refuse: the reasons would disclose information about another person that cannot practicably be removed (s 269(9)); written notice within 28 days`
    `the decision-maker may refuse: a public interest certificate covers information the statement cannot leave out without misleading (s 269(10)); written notice within 28 days`
    `the decision-maker must give me a statement of reasons within 28 days after receiving my request (s 269(2)), and tell me of anything removed (s 269(3)-(5))`
    `not covered: the decision is not reviewable, so Part 10 Division 3 does not apply`

@export I have asked the decision-maker for reasons for a reviewable decision: must they give them, or can they refuse (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 269)? The decision-maker must give a statement of reasons within 28 days after receiving the request, unless your interests are not affected (must refuse), you already have reasons, your request was late, the reasons would disclose another person's information that cannot be removed, or a public interest certificate applies (may refuse). A refusal needs written notice with the reason.
GIVEN `reviewable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is one an Act or instrument lets you take to the Administrative Review Tribunal
      `my interests are affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision affects your interests (for an organisation, see the question on asking for reasons)
      `already given reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already been given a statement of reasons for this decision (in the decision document or separately)
      `set out in a document given to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was set out in a document given to you
      `document given on` IS A DATE @desc The day that document was given to you (if there was none, any date; it is not used)
      `requested on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your written request for reasons
      `late as a fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the decision was not set out in a document given to you: whether the decision-maker considers your request was not made within a reasonable time after the decision
      `would disclose another person's information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reasons would disclose information about another person that the decision-maker cannot reasonably practicably remove
      `certificate makes it misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 272 public interest certificate covers information in the reasons, and the statement would be false or misleading without it
GIVETH A `What happens to my request for reasons`
`What happens to my request for reasons question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `reviewable`
              THEN `not covered: the decision is not reviewable, so Part 10 Division 3 does not apply`
           IF NOT `my interests are affected`
              THEN `the decision-maker must refuse: my interests are not affected (s 269(6)); written notice of refusal within 28 days (s 269(11), (12))`
           IF `already given reasons`
              THEN `the decision-maker may refuse: I have already been given a statement of reasons (s 269(7)); no refusal notice is needed`
           IF `late`
              THEN `the decision-maker may refuse: my request was late (s 269(8)); a refusal needs written notice within 28 days (s 269(11), (12))`
           IF `would disclose another person's information`
              THEN `the decision-maker may refuse: the reasons would disclose information about another person that cannot practicably be removed (s 269(9)); written notice within 28 days`
           IF `certificate makes it misleading`
              THEN `the decision-maker may refuse: a public interest certificate covers information the statement cannot leave out without misleading (s 269(10)); written notice within 28 days`
           OTHERWISE `the decision-maker must give me a statement of reasons within 28 days after receiving my request (s 269(2)), and tell me of anything removed (s 269(3)-(5))`
    WHERE
        `late` MEANS
            IF `set out in a document given to me`
            THEN `the days from` `document given on` `to` `requested on` GREATER THAN 28
            ELSE `late as a fact`

-- s 269(2) and (12)(b): the statement, or the written refusal, "within 28 days after receiving the request".
@export By when must the decision-maker give me a statement of reasons, or tell me in writing that my request is refused (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 269(2), (12))? Both are due within 28 days after the decision-maker receives the request.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker received your written request for reasons
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker answer my request for reasons question` `received on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `received on`

-- The duty in s 269(2), (11), (12) as a regulative rule, counted in days from the day the request is received.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant party` `Review step`
`s 269 -- the decision-maker must answer my request for reasons within 28 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the decision-maker`
    MUST `answer my request for reasons (a statement of reasons or a written refusal)`
    WITHIN 28
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the decision-maker` BECAUSE "no statement of reasons or written refusal within 28 days after the request was received: I may apply to the Tribunal for a decision whether reasons should be given (s 270(1)(b))"

GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 269 -- an answer on this day is in time` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 269 -- the decision-maker must answer my request for reasons within 28 days` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the decision-maker` `answer my request for reasons (a statement of reasons or a written refusal)` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Where my request for reasons stands` IS ONE OF
    `answered in time`
    `not yet due: the decision-maker has until the 28th day after receiving my request`
    `late: no answer within 28 days; I may apply to the Tribunal for a decision whether reasons should be given (s 270(1)(b), (2))`

@export I asked for reasons for a reviewable decision: has the decision-maker answered in time (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 269, 270)? The decision-maker must give reasons or a written refusal within 28 days after receiving the request. If they do not, you may apply to the Tribunal for a decision on whether they should give you reasons.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker received your written request for reasons
      `answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given a statement of reasons or a written refusal
      `answered on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given it (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have had no answer)
GIVETH A `Where my request for reasons stands`
`Where does my request for reasons stand question` MEANS
    IF `answered`
    THEN IF `s 269 -- an answer on this day is in time` (`the days from` `received on` `to` `answered on`)
         THEN `answered in time`
         ELSE `late: no answer within 28 days; I may apply to the Tribunal for a decision whether reasons should be given (s 270(1)(b), (2))`
    ELSE IF `the days from` `received on` `to` `today` AT MOST 28
         THEN `not yet due: the decision-maker has until the 28th day after receiving my request`
         ELSE `late: no answer within 28 days; I may apply to the Tribunal for a decision whether reasons should be given (s 270(1)(b), (2))`

-- s 270(1): applies if a person requests reasons under s 268 "and: (a) the person is given notice that the request is
-- refused; or (b) the person is not given a statement in response to the request within 28 days after the request is
-- received." (2): "The person may apply to the Tribunal for a decision about whether the decision-maker should give the
-- person the statement of reasons." JUST FALSE where neither (a) nor (b) holds or I made no request ("applies if": the
-- text's no).
@export Can I ask the Administrative Review Tribunal to decide whether the decision-maker must give me reasons (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 270)? Yes, if you asked the decision-maker for reasons and were refused, or got no statement within 28 days after they received your request.
GIVEN `I requested reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the decision-maker in writing for a statement of reasons for a reviewable decision
      `refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given notice that your request is refused
      `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker received your request
      `statement given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given a statement of reasons in response
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement was given (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the Tribunal to make the decision-maker give reasons question` MEANS
    IF `I requested reasons` AND (`refused` OR `no statement within 28 days`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `no statement within 28 days` MEANS
            IF `statement given`
            THEN `the days from` `received on` `to` `given on` GREATER THAN 28
            ELSE `the days from` `received on` `to` `today` GREATER THAN 28

-- s 271(1): applies if "(a) ... a person requests ... a statement of reasons ... under section 268; [and] ... is given a
-- statement of reasons ... under section 269 or 270; or (b) a person applies to the Tribunal for review of a reviewable
-- decision for which the decision-maker has given the person a statement of reasons." (2): the person "may apply to the
-- Tribunal for a decision about whether the statement contains adequate information about" findings on material questions
-- of fact, the evidence, the reasons.
@export I have a statement of reasons that does not explain enough: can I ask the Administrative Review Tribunal to decide whether it is adequate (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 271)? Yes, if you asked for and were given a statement under s 269 or s 270, or if you have applied for review of a decision for which the decision-maker gave you reasons. The Tribunal decides whether it adequately covers the findings of fact, the evidence and the reasons.
GIVEN `requested and given under s 269 or 270` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for reasons under s 268 and were given a statement under s 269 or s 270
      `applied for review and given reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Tribunal for review of the decision and the decision-maker has given you a statement of reasons for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the Tribunal about an inadequate statement of reasons question` MEANS
    IF `requested and given under s 269 or 270` OR `applied for review and given reasons`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 270(4), s 271(4): if the Tribunal decides reasons (or an additional statement) must be given, the decision-maker must
-- give it "within 28 days after the Tribunal notifies the decision-maker of the Tribunal's decision".
@export The Administrative Review Tribunal has decided that the decision-maker must give me reasons, or a fuller statement: by when must I get it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 270(4), 271(4))? Within 28 days after the Tribunal notifies the decision-maker of its decision.
GIVEN `decision-maker notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal notified the decision-maker of its decision that reasons (or an additional statement) must be given
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker give reasons the Tribunal has required question` `decision-maker notified on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `decision-maker notified on`

§§ `Applying for review (Part 3 Divisions 2 and 3; s 108(3))`

-- s 17(1): "A person whose interests are affected by a reviewable decision may apply to the Tribunal for review of the
-- decision." (2): the Commonwealth or a statutory body may apply too. s 97: the Tribunal "must dismiss an application if
-- ... the Tribunal is satisfied that the decision is not reviewable". s 108(3): "Application may not be made to the
-- Tribunal for review of the reviewable decision as varied or the decision made in substitution" (Part 5A aside).
-- JUST TRUE: I may apply. JUST FALSE: the decision is the Tribunal's own varied or substituted decision (s 108(3)), or is
-- not reviewable (s 97, the text's no). NOTHING: my interests are not affected (s 17 grants to affected persons and says
-- no more; fork F2).
@export Can I apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of a government decision (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 12, 15, 17, 97, 108(3))? A person whose interests are affected may apply, if an Act or legislative instrument provides for Tribunal review of the decision (look at the law the decision was made under). An organisation is affected if the decision concerns a matter in its objects. A decision the Tribunal itself varied or substituted cannot be taken back to it this way (see second review and the guidance and appeals panel).
GIVEN `review of this decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or legislative instrument provides for applying to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of this decision (the notice of decision usually says)
      `review under this power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or instrument provides for Tribunal review of decisions made under the power used (even if the person who made this decision was not authorised)
      `affected as a fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision affects your interests
      `an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an organisation or association (incorporated or not) rather than an individual
      `in its objects then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are an organisation: whether the decision relates to a matter in your objects or purposes at the time it was made
      `since removed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are an organisation: whether that matter has since been removed from your objects or purposes
      `the Tribunal's varied or substituted decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision you want reviewed is one the Tribunal itself varied, or made in substitution for the original, on an earlier review
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the Tribunal for review question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Tribunal's varied or substituted decision`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF NOT (`s 12 -- a reviewable decision` `review of this decision` `review under this power`)
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE IF `ss 15, 17 -- my interests are affected` `affected as a fact` `an organisation` `in its objects then` `since removed`
                        THEN JUST TRUE
                        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 16: if "(a) a decision of a person not to do a thing is a reviewable decision; and (b) an Act or an instrument ...
-- provides for a quantified period within which the person is required or permitted to do the thing; and (c) the person
-- does not do the thing within the period; the person is taken ... to have made a decision at the end of the period not to
-- do the thing." Answers the day the refusal is deemed made; NOTHING where s 16 does not deem one (with no quantified
-- period, the note points to the Ombudsman Act 1976 s 10, not encoded).
@export The decision-maker has not acted on my claim within the time the law gives them: is a refusal taken to have been made that I can take to the Administrative Review Tribunal, and on what day (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 16)? If a decision not to do the thing is reviewable, and the law sets a quantified period to do it, a refusal is deemed made at the end of that period.
GIVEN `a refusal would be reviewable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a decision not to do the thing you asked for (for example, not to determine your claim) is one you could take to the Tribunal
      `the law sets a quantified period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or instrument sets a fixed period (for example 90 days) within which the decision-maker must or may do the thing
      `the period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that period
      `done within the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker did the thing within the period
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Is a refusal taken to have been made because time ran out question` MEANS
    IF `a refusal would be reviewable` AND `the law sets a quantified period` AND (NOT `done within the period`)
    THEN JUST `the period ends on`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 18(3): "Rules made for the purposes of subsection (1) must not prescribe a period ending before the day that is 28 days
-- after the day the decision is made." The earliest day the Tribunal's rules may make my time to apply end.
@export What is the earliest day my time to apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal can end under the Tribunal's rules (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 18(3))? The rules may not prescribe a period ending before the day 28 days after the day the decision is made. The law the decision was made under may set a different period, which this floor does not bind.
GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the earliest my time to apply can end question` `decision made on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `decision made on`

-- s 18(1): "An application to the Tribunal for review of a decision must be made within the period prescribed by the
-- rules." (note: the enabling legislation "may specify a different period"). (2): subject to s 19. The rules are not
-- deposited: the days they (or the enabling law) allow, and the day the count starts, are inputs. As a regulative rule:
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant party` `Review step`
`s 18 -- I must apply within the prescribed period` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the applicant`
    DO `apply to the Tribunal for review`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the applicant` BECAUSE "no application within the prescribed period: the Tribunal may still extend the period, even after it has expired (s 19)"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 18 -- an application on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 18 -- I must apply within the prescribed period` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `I, the applicant` `apply to the Tribunal for review` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Who sets my time to apply` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal's rules (s 18(1))`
    `the Act or instrument the decision was made under`
    `no law sets a period`

DECLARE `Am I in time to apply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: within the prescribed period (s 18(1))`
    `not yet late: the period has not ended`
    `the Tribunal has extended the period (s 19), and I am within it`
    `out of time: I need the Tribunal to extend the period (s 19); it may do so even after the period has expired`
    `no period applies: in time unless the Tribunal considers I did not apply within a reasonable time (s 20)`
    `not covered: the rules' period as entered ends before the earliest end the rules may set (s 18(3))`

@export Am I in time to apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 18, 19, 20)? An application must be made within the period the Tribunal's rules prescribe (never ending before 28 days after the decision), or a different period set by the law the decision was made under. If you are late, the Tribunal may extend the time, even after it has expired. Enter the number of days the rules or the law allow and the day the count starts, as the rules are not part of this encoding.
GIVEN `who sets the period` IS A `Who sets my time to apply` @desc Who sets your time to apply: the Tribunal's rules, the Act or instrument the decision was made under, or no law at all
      `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made
      `count starts on` IS A DATE @desc The day from which the period is counted (for example the day you were given the decision), as the rules or the law say
      `days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the rules or the law allow for applying, counted from that day
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet applied)
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has extended your time to apply
      `extended last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the extended period (if not extended, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Am I in time to apply`
`Am I in time to apply question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `who sets the period` EQUALS `no law sets a period`
              THEN `no period applies: in time unless the Tribunal considers I did not apply within a reasonable time (s 20)`
           IF `who sets the period` EQUALS `the Tribunal's rules (s 18(1))` AND `the day` (`What is the earliest my time to apply can end question` `decision made on`) `is after` `the last day`
              THEN `not covered: the rules' period as entered ends before the earliest end the rules may set (s 18(3))`
           IF `s 18 -- an application on this day is in time` `days allowed` (`the days from` `count starts on` `to` `the day of applying`)
              THEN IF `I have applied`
                   THEN `yes: within the prescribed period (s 18(1))`
                   ELSE `not yet late: the period has not ended`
           IF `extended` AND `the day` `the day of applying` `is on or before` `extended last day`
              THEN `the Tribunal has extended the period (s 19), and I am within it`
           OTHERWISE `out of time: I need the Tribunal to extend the period (s 19); it may do so even after the period has expired`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the date` `days allowed` `days after` `count starts on`
        `the day of applying` MEANS
            IF `I have applied`
            THEN `applied on`
            ELSE `today`

-- s 19(1)-(3): I may apply to extend; "The Tribunal may, by order, extend the period if the Tribunal considers that it is
-- reasonable in all the circumstances to do so", "even if it has expired". (4): "must not extend the period if: (a)
-- section 102 permits the applicant to apply ... to reinstate an application ...; or (b) the Tribunal refuses an
-- application by the applicant under section 102 to reinstate". (7): if someone notified under (5) opposes, the Tribunal
-- "must not extend the period unless" both sides have had "a reasonable opportunity to adduce evidence and make
-- submissions". JUST TRUE: the Tribunal may extend. JUST FALSE: s 19(4), s 19(7), or the Tribunal does not consider an
-- extension reasonable ("if": the text's no).
@export I am late: can the Administrative Review Tribunal extend my time to apply for review (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 19)? The Tribunal may extend the period, even after it has expired, if it considers that reasonable in all the circumstances. It cannot if you could instead apply to reinstate an earlier application for review of the same decision, or if it has refused such a reinstatement; and if someone opposes, it must first give both of you a reasonable opportunity to give evidence and make submissions.
GIVEN `reinstatement available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether s 102 lets you apply to reinstate an earlier application of yours for review of the same decision that was dismissed
      `reinstatement refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has refused your application under s 102 to reinstate an earlier application for review of the same decision
      `someone opposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person notified of your extension application told the Tribunal (within 14 days) that they oppose it
      `opportunity given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If someone opposed: whether the Tribunal gave you and them a reasonable opportunity to give evidence and make submissions on the extension
      `the Tribunal considers it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal considers an extension reasonable in all the circumstances
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Tribunal extend my time to apply question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `reinstatement available` OR `reinstatement refused`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `someone opposed` AND (NOT `opportunity given`)
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE IF `the Tribunal considers it reasonable`
                        THEN JUST TRUE
                        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 19(6): "A person notified under subsection (5) may notify the Tribunal within 14 days that the person wishes to oppose
-- the extension application."
@export By when can a person the Tribunal has told about my application for more time to apply say that they oppose it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 19(6))? Within 14 days after they are notified.
GIVEN `they were notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the other person was notified of your extension application
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can someone oppose my extension application question` `they were notified on` MEANS `the date` 14 `days after` `they were notified on`

-- s 20(1): if the application "is not required by law to be made within any particular period" and "the Tribunal considers
-- that the application was not made within a reasonable time after the making of the decision; the Tribunal must dismiss
-- the application." (2): not "if the Tribunal considers that there are special circumstances that justify" review. (3):
-- the Tribunal must have regard to when I became aware of the decision, and to any period that would have bound someone
-- else. JUST TRUE: must dismiss. JUST FALSE: special circumstances, or a reasonable time. NOTHING: a period applies (s 18).
@export No time limit applies to my application: must the Administrative Review Tribunal dismiss it for delay (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 20)? If no law sets a period, the Tribunal must dismiss the application if it considers it was not made within a reasonable time after the decision (having regard to when you became aware of the decision), unless it considers special circumstances justify the review.
GIVEN `a period applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law (the Tribunal's rules or the law the decision was made under) sets a period for applying
      `the Tribunal considers it unreasonably late` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal considers your application was not made within a reasonable time after the decision (having regard to when you became aware of it)
      `special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal considers special circumstances justify reviewing the decision
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Tribunal dismiss my application for delay question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `a period applies`
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `the Tribunal considers it unreasonably late` AND (NOT `special circumstances`)
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `After I apply: reasons and documents, the decision, a stay (Part 3 Division 4)`

-- s 23: "Within 28 days after the Tribunal notifies the decision-maker ... of an application for review ..., the
-- decision-maker must give the Tribunal: (a) a statement of reasons for the decision; and (b) a copy of every other
-- document" in its possession or control and relevant. s 27(1): it "must give a copy ... to each other party ... within
-- the same period"; (2): not if a s 91 certificate covers it. s 28: the Tribunal may shorten or extend the period (even
-- after it has expired), or order that it not be given; (9) not "in the circumstances specified in the practice
-- directions". s 29: while an application under s 70 to restrict disclosure is pending, not unless the Tribunal orders it.
-- s 131G: s 23 "does not apply in relation to an application for second review". As a regulative rule, days counted from
-- the Tribunal's notice to the decision-maker:
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant party` `Review step`
`ss 23, 27 -- the decision-maker must give me the reasons and documents` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the decision-maker`
    MUST `give me the statement of reasons and the relevant documents`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the decision-maker` BECAUSE "the statement of reasons and relevant documents were not given within the period (ss 23, 27(1)); I may ask the Tribunal for orders (s 79), and it may proceed under s 106(4)"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 23, 27 -- documents given on this day are in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`ss 23, 27 -- the decision-maker must give me the reasons and documents` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the decision-maker` `give me the statement of reasons and the relevant documents` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Where the decision-maker's reasons and documents stand` IS ONE OF
    `not required: s 23 does not apply to second review (s 131G)`
    `not required: the Tribunal ordered they not be given (s 28(8))`
    `not required: the practice directions excuse them in these circumstances (s 28(9))`
    `on hold: an application to restrict their disclosure is pending; not due until the Tribunal orders (s 29)`
    `not to me: a public interest certificate under s 91 covers them (s 27(2)); the Tribunal still gets them`
    `given in time`
    `not yet due`
    `late: the decision-maker has not given them within the period (ss 23, 27(1))`

@export I have applied for review: has the decision-maker given me (and the Tribunal) its statement of reasons and the relevant documents in time (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 23, 27, 28, 29, 131G)? The decision-maker must give them within 28 days after the Tribunal notifies it of your application, and give you copies within the same period. The Tribunal may shorten the period (if a party could suffer hardship), extend it, or excuse the documents.
GIVEN `second review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is for second review of a Tribunal decision on a social services decision (Part 5A)
      `the Tribunal ordered they not be given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has ordered the decision-maker not to give the statement or documents
      `the practice directions excuse them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's practice directions say they need not be given in your circumstances
      `a disclosure application is pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has applied for an order restricting disclosure of the statement or documents and given them to the Tribunal, and the Tribunal has not yet ordered them given
      `a public interest certificate covers them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a public interest certificate under s 91 covers disclosing them in the proceeding
      `decision-maker notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal notified the decision-maker of your application
      `the Tribunal changed the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has shortened or extended the period
      `changed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period as shortened or extended (if not changed, any date; it is not used)
      `given to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the statement and documents
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given them (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not been given them)
GIVETH A `Where the decision-maker's reasons and documents stand`
`Where are the decision-maker's reasons and documents question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `second review`
              THEN `not required: s 23 does not apply to second review (s 131G)`
           IF `a disclosure application is pending`
              THEN `on hold: an application to restrict their disclosure is pending; not due until the Tribunal orders (s 29)`
           IF `the Tribunal ordered they not be given`
              THEN `not required: the Tribunal ordered they not be given (s 28(8))`
           IF `the practice directions excuse them`
              THEN `not required: the practice directions excuse them in these circumstances (s 28(9))`
           IF `a public interest certificate covers them`
              THEN `not to me: a public interest certificate under s 91 covers them (s 27(2)); the Tribunal still gets them`
           IF `given to me`
              THEN IF `ss 23, 27 -- documents given on this day are in time` `the days allowed` (`the days from` `decision-maker notified on` `to` `given on`)
                   THEN `given in time`
                   ELSE `late: the decision-maker has not given them within the period (ss 23, 27(1))`
           IF `the days from` `decision-maker notified on` `to` `today` AT MOST `the days allowed`
              THEN `not yet due`
           OTHERWISE `late: the decision-maker has not given them within the period (ss 23, 27(1))`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS
            IF `the Tribunal changed the period`
            THEN `the days from` `decision-maker notified on` `to` `changed last day`
            ELSE 28

-- s 31(1): "After an application is made to the Tribunal for review of a reviewable decision, the decision may not be
-- altered otherwise than by the Tribunal." (2): unless "(a) ... the parties ... consent ... [and] the Tribunal, by order,
-- consents ...; or (b) the decision is remitted to the decision-maker under section 85." Alter = vary, set aside, or set
-- aside and substitute (s 31(3)). JUST FALSE is the text's flat no. NOTHING before any application (s 31 is silent).
@export I have applied for review: can the decision-maker change the decision themselves while my review is on (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 31)? No, unless all the parties consent and the Tribunal consents by order, or the Tribunal remits the decision to the decision-maker for reconsideration (s 85).
GIVEN `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Tribunal for review of the decision
      `all parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties to the review (you included) consent to the change
      `the Tribunal consents by order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has consented to the change by order
      `remitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has remitted the decision to the decision-maker to reconsider (s 85)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the decision-maker change the decision while my review is on question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `I have applied`
              THEN NOTHING
           IF (`all parties consent` AND `the Tribunal consents by order`) OR `remitted`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 32(1): applying "does not affect the operation of the decision or prevent the taking of action to implement" it. (2):
-- on a party's application the Tribunal "may make an order staying or otherwise affecting the operation or implementation
-- of the decision if the Tribunal considers that it is desirable to do so for the purpose of ensuring the effectiveness of
-- the review". (4): until the Tribunal's decision comes into operation; (5): or the end of a period the order states, if
-- earlier; (6): it may be varied or revoked. (9): an order made without hearing me takes effect when I am given notice.
-- JUST TRUE: stayed now. JUST FALSE: not stayed (s 32(1), the text's no; or the order has ended).
@export Does applying to the Administrative Review Tribunal stop the decision from operating or being carried out (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 32)? No: the decision keeps operating unless the Tribunal, on a party's application, orders a stay to make the review effective. A stay lasts until the Tribunal's own decision comes into operation, or the end of any shorter period the order states, and may be varied or revoked.
GIVEN `a stay order was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has made an order staying or otherwise affecting the operation or implementation of the decision
      `revoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has revoked that order
      `the order states a period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order says it applies for a period
      `the period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that period (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `the Tribunal's decision is in operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's decision on your review has come into operation
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the decision I applied about stayed question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `a stay order was made`) OR `revoked` OR `the Tribunal's decision is in operation`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the order states a period` AND `the day` `today` `is after` `the period ends on`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `In the proceeding: representation (Part 4 Division 5)`

-- s 66(1): "A party to a proceeding in the Tribunal may choose another person to represent the party". (2): a person
-- "required or permitted to appear" may, "with the permission of the Tribunal", choose a representative. (3): the Tribunal
-- may order that a person is not to be represented by a representative with a conflict of interest, not acting in their
-- best interests, presenting a safety or an unacceptable privacy risk, or "otherwise impeding the Tribunal".
@export Can I choose someone (a lawyer, an advocate, a family member) to represent me before the Administrative Review Tribunal (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 66)? A party may choose anyone to represent them. Someone who is not a party but must or may appear needs the Tribunal's permission. The Tribunal may order that a particular representative not act (conflict of interest, not acting in your best interests, a safety or privacy risk, or impeding the Tribunal); you can then choose another.
GIVEN `I am a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceeding (an applicant always is)
      `the Tribunal permits it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not a party but must or may appear: whether the Tribunal permits you to be represented
      `the Tribunal ordered this representative out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has ordered that you are not to be represented by this particular person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I choose someone to represent me question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Tribunal ordered this representative out`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `I am a party` OR `the Tribunal permits it`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `Dismissal, withdrawal and reinstatement (Part 4 Division 8 Subdivision A; s 84) [if my application is ended]`

-- s 20 (must, no period applies), s 97 (must, "satisfied that the decision is not reviewable"), s 84(4) (may: no one can
-- continue after the applicant's death, bankruptcy or winding up, or no application to continue within 3 months), s 96
-- (may: "consent of the parties"), s 98 (may: fee "not paid by the time prescribed by the rules"), s 99 (may: failed to
-- appear, having "received appropriate notice"), s 100 (may: failed "within a reasonable time" to proceed or to comply),
-- s 101 (may: "frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance", "no reasonable prospects of success", abuse of
-- process). The Tribunal's satisfactions are inputs. s 65(2) and s 83(2), (4): the applicant cannot be removed as a party
-- (only the application dismissed or withdrawn).
DECLARE `Can my application be dismissed` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it must be: the decision is not reviewable (s 97)`
    `yes, it must be: no period applies and it was not made within a reasonable time, with no special circumstances (s 20)`
    `it may be: the Tribunal has a power to dismiss on these facts (ss 84(4), 96, 98, 99, 100, 101)`
    `no: none of ss 20, 84, 96 to 101 gives a ground on these facts (and as applicant I cannot be removed as a party: ss 65(2), 83(2), (4))`

@export Can the Administrative Review Tribunal dismiss my application for review without deciding it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 20, 84, 96-101)? It must dismiss if the decision is not reviewable, or if no time limit applied and you applied unreasonably late without special circumstances. It may dismiss if the parties consent, the fee is not paid in time, you fail to appear after proper notice, you fail to proceed or comply within a reasonable time, the application is frivolous, vexatious or hopeless, or after the applicant's death or bankruptcy no one continues it.
GIVEN `not reviewable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the decision is not reviewable by it
      `must dismiss for delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether s 20 requires dismissal (no period applied; the Tribunal considers you applied unreasonably late; no special circumstances) -- see the question on dismissal for delay
      `parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the participating parties consent to dismissal
      `fee unpaid in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a fee you must pay for the application was not paid by the time the rules prescribe
      `failed to appear after notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to appear at a hearing, directions hearing or dispute resolution process, and the Tribunal is satisfied you had appropriate notice of it
      `failed to proceed or comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed, within a reasonable time, to proceed with the application or to comply with the Act or a Tribunal order
      `frivolous or hopeless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the application is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, has no reasonable prospects of success, or is an abuse of process
      `no one continues after the applicant's death or bankruptcy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the applicant died, became bankrupt or was wound up or put into administration, and either no one can continue the application or no one applied to continue within 3 months after the Tribunal became aware
GIVETH A `Can my application be dismissed`
`Can the Tribunal dismiss my application question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `not reviewable`
              THEN `yes, it must be: the decision is not reviewable (s 97)`
           IF `must dismiss for delay`
              THEN `yes, it must be: no period applies and it was not made within a reasonable time, with no special circumstances (s 20)`
           IF `parties consent` OR `fee unpaid in time` OR `failed to appear after notice` OR `failed to proceed or comply` OR `frivolous or hopeless` OR `no one continues after the applicant's death or bankruptcy`
              THEN `it may be: the Tribunal has a power to dismiss on these facts (ss 84(4), 96, 98, 99, 100, 101)`
           OTHERWISE `no: none of ss 20, 84, 96 to 101 gives a ground on these facts (and as applicant I cannot be removed as a party: ss 65(2), 83(2), (4))`

-- s 102(5): "A party to the proceeding ... may apply to the Tribunal for reinstatement of the application on the grounds of
-- error within 28 days after the party receives notice that the application is dismissed (or such longer period as the
-- Tribunal, in special circumstances, allows)." (7): if dismissed under s 95 (withdrawn), s 99 (did not appear) or s 100
-- (failed to proceed or comply), a party may apply "within 28 days after the party receives notice" (or a longer period
-- allowed). (8): "the applicant cannot apply if the application is dismissed under section 95". s 95(2): a withdrawn
-- application "is taken to have" been dismissed. JUST TRUE: I may apply now. JUST FALSE: out of time with no longer period
-- allowed (a period the text fixes), or no route on these facts (s 102(8), or (7)'s "If").
DECLARE `How my application was ended` IS ONE OF
    `I withdrew it (s 95)`
    `dismissed by consent of the parties (s 96)`
    `dismissed because I did not appear (s 99)`
    `dismissed because I did not proceed or comply (s 100)`
    `dismissed on another ground (ss 20, 84, 97, 98, 101)`

@export My application to the Administrative Review Tribunal was dismissed (or I withdrew it): can I apply to have it reinstated (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 102)? Any party may apply within 28 days after receiving notice of the dismissal, on the ground that it was dismissed in error. If it was dismissed because a party did not appear or did not proceed or comply, a party may also apply without showing error; but an applicant who withdrew can use only the error ground. The Tribunal may allow a longer period in special circumstances.
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my application was ended` @desc How the application came to an end
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were the applicant (rather than another party)
      `I say it was an error` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are applying on the ground that the application was dismissed in error
      `notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice that the application was dismissed
      `applying on` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or would apply) for reinstatement
      `longer period allowed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has, in special circumstances, allowed you a longer period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to have my application reinstated question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`the error route` OR `the other route`)
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the days from` `notice received on` `to` `applying on` AT MOST 28 OR `longer period allowed`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the error route` MEANS `I say it was an error`
        `the other route` MEANS
            CONSIDER `how it ended`
            WHEN `I withdrew it (s 95)` THEN NOT `I am the applicant`
            WHEN `dismissed because I did not appear (s 99)` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `dismissed because I did not proceed or comply (s 100)` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 102(5), (7): "within 28 days after the party receives notice that the application is dismissed".
@export By when must I apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal to reinstate my dismissed application (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 102(5), (7))? Within 28 days after you receive notice of the dismissal, unless the Tribunal allows a longer period in special circumstances.
GIVEN `notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice that the application was dismissed
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for reinstatement question` `notice received on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `notice received on`

-- s 84(1): applies if the applicant dies, becomes bankrupt, is wound up or ceases to exist, or comes under liquidation or
-- administration, and "the Tribunal considers that the applicant cannot continue". (2): "(a) a legal personal
-- representative, executor, administrator, liquidator or trustee of the applicant ...; (b) a person who would have been
-- entitled to apply for review of the decision at the time the substantive application was made" may apply to continue.
-- Seat: the person stepping in. JUST TRUE: I may apply. JUST FALSE: I am neither (2)(a) nor (b) (a closed list).
-- NOTHING: s 84 is not engaged.
@export The person who applied to the Administrative Review Tribunal has died, become bankrupt or gone into liquidation or administration: can I apply to continue their application (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 84)? If the Tribunal considers the applicant cannot continue, their legal personal representative, executor, administrator, liquidator or trustee, or anyone who could have applied for review of the decision when the application was made, may apply to continue it.
GIVEN `the event happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the applicant died, became bankrupt, was wound up or ceased to exist, or became subject to liquidation or administration
      `the Tribunal considers they cannot continue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal considers the applicant cannot continue because of that event
      `I represent them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the applicant's legal personal representative, executor, administrator, liquidator or trustee
      `I could have applied myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would have been entitled to apply for review of the decision when the application was made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I continue a review after the applicant died or became bankrupt question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`the event happened` AND `the Tribunal considers they cannot continue`)
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `I represent them` OR `I could have applied myself`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 84(4)(b): the Tribunal may dismiss if "no application to continue ... is made within 3 months after the Tribunal becomes
-- aware of the event". Counted with the Acts Interpretation Act month (art-dates).
@export By when must someone apply to continue a review after the applicant's death, bankruptcy or winding up, before the Administrative Review Tribunal may dismiss it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 84(4)(b))? Within 3 months after the Tribunal becomes aware of the event.
GIVEN `the Tribunal became aware on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal became aware of the death, bankruptcy, winding up, liquidation or administration
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must someone apply to continue the review question` `the Tribunal became aware on` MEANS `the last day of` 3 `months after` `the Tribunal became aware on`

§§ `The Tribunal's decision (Part 4 Divisions 8 and 10)`

-- s 111(2): on a review decision under s 105 (not one agreed under s 103(2)), the Tribunal must give each party, in
-- writing, the decision, a statement of reasons, and notice of the rights to apply to the guidance and appeals panel, for
-- second review, and to appeal to the Federal Court. (3): "by the time specified in the practice directions. If the
-- practice directions do not specify a time, ... within 28 days after the day the Tribunal's decision is made." (4A):
-- lateness of the notices of rights does not affect validity. NOTHING for an agreed decision (s 111(1)).
@export By when must the Administrative Review Tribunal give me its written decision and reasons on my review (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 111)? By the time the practice directions specify, or if they specify none, within 28 days after the day the decision is made. It must also tell you of your rights to go to the guidance and appeals panel, to second review and to the Federal Court. Not for a decision agreed by the parties.
GIVEN `agreed by the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made in the terms the parties agreed (s 103(2))
      `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made its decision
      `practice directions set a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's practice directions specify the time for giving the decision and reasons
      `practice direction last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day under the practice directions (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the Tribunal give me its decision and reasons question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `agreed by the parties`
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `practice directions set a time`
              THEN JUST `practice direction last day`
           OTHERWISE JUST (`the date` 28 `days after` `decision made on`)

-- s 112(5): for a decision finalising a proceeding other than a review decision under s 105 (and other than ss 94, 96,
-- 103(2)), "If the Tribunal has not given a party ... a statement of reasons ..., the party may, within 28 days after the
-- day the Tribunal's decision is made, request" one, in writing (6). (7): "Within 28 days after receiving the request, the
-- Tribunal must give the party a statement of reasons". NOTHING where I already have a statement.
@export The Administrative Review Tribunal ended my case by a decision other than a decision on the review (for example dismissing my application) without written reasons: by when can I ask for them (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 112(5), (6))? In writing, within 28 days after the day the decision is made. The Tribunal must then give reasons within 28 days after receiving the request.
GIVEN `already given a statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has already given you a written statement of reasons for this decision
      `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made the decision
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when can I ask the Tribunal for reasons for a decision ending my case question` MEANS
    IF `already given a statement`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the date` 28 `days after` `decision made on`)

@export I have asked the Administrative Review Tribunal for reasons for a decision ending my case: by when must it give them (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 112(7))? Within 28 days after it receives your request.
GIVEN `request received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal received your written request for reasons
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the Tribunal give reasons I asked for question` `request received on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `request received on`

-- s 107(1): "comes into operation when it is given to the parties". (2): a later time the decision specifies. (3): if a s 32
-- stay of the reviewable decision "is in force immediately before the Tribunal makes a decision under section 105", the
-- Tribunal's decision is stayed until "(c) if a relevant appeal is made -- the later of ... the end of the appeal period
-- [and] the completion of the relevant appeal; or (d) in any other case -- the end of the appeal period". (4): a late appeal
-- is not counted. (5): not for an agreed decision under s 103(2). (6): the Tribunal, the Federal Court or the Federal
-- Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) "may order that a decision of the Tribunal comes into operation at a
-- different time". NOTHING: stayed pending an appeal not yet completed (the day is not yet known).
@export When does the Administrative Review Tribunal's decision on my review take effect (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 107)? When it is given to the parties, or a later time the decision specifies. But if the original decision was stayed when the Tribunal decided, the Tribunal's decision waits until the end of the appeal period, or, if an appeal is made in time, until that appeal is completed if later. The Tribunal or a court may order a different time.
GIVEN `given to the parties on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was given to the parties
      `a later time specified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision says it comes into operation at a later time
      `specified day` IS A DATE @desc That later day (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `stay in force when decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Tribunal stay order (s 32) on the original decision was in force immediately before the Tribunal decided
      `agreed by the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made in the terms the parties agreed (s 103(2))
      `end of the appeal period` IS A DATE @desc The last day for appealing to the Federal Court (or, if a guidance and appeals panel application could be made and its period ends later, that later day) -- see the question on appealing to the Federal Court
      `an appeal made in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an appeal to the Federal Court (or an application to the guidance and appeals panel, if one could be made) was made within its period
      `the appeal is completed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that appeal has been determined, discontinued, dismissed or disposed of (or the panel application refused, or the panel's decision has come into operation)
      `completed on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was completed (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `a different time ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal, the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) has ordered that the decision come into operation at a different time
      `ordered day` IS A DATE @desc That day (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`When does the Tribunal's decision come into operation question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `a different time ordered`
              THEN JUST `ordered day`
           IF `stay in force when decided` AND (NOT `agreed by the parties`)
              THEN IF `an appeal made in time`
                   THEN IF `the appeal is completed`
                        THEN JUST (`the later of` `end of the appeal period` `and` `completed on`)
                        ELSE NOTHING
                   ELSE JUST `end of the appeal period`
           IF `a later time specified`
              THEN JUST (`the later of` `specified day` `and` `given to the parties on`)
           OTHERWISE JUST `given to the parties on`

-- s 108(4): when the Tribunal's decision to vary or set aside comes into operation, the varied or substituted decision "has
-- effect, or is taken to have had effect, from the time at which the reviewable decision has or had effect." (5): "does
-- not apply if the Tribunal orders otherwise." JUST TRUE: it reaches back. NOTHING: the Tribunal ordered otherwise (from
-- when is then for the order; the Act does not say). JUST FALSE: the Tribunal affirmed (s 108(1): applies only to a
-- variation or substitution).
@export The Administrative Review Tribunal changed or replaced the decision in my favour: does the new decision reach back to when the original decision took effect (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 108(4), (5))? Yes, once the Tribunal's decision comes into operation, the varied or substituted decision has effect from the time the original decision had effect, unless the Tribunal orders otherwise.
GIVEN `varied or substituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal varied the decision, or set it aside and made a decision in its place
      `the Tribunal ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal ordered that the decision is not to reach back to when the original took effect
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the changed decision reach back question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `varied or substituted`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the Tribunal ordered otherwise`
              THEN NOTHING
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `The guidance and appeals panel (Part 5 Division 3) [if I want the Tribunal's decision looked at again]`

-- s 123(1): "Application may be made to the President to refer a decision of the Tribunal under section 105 ... to the
-- guidance and appeals panel." (2): "by a person who was at any time a party to the proceeding". (4): "does not apply" to a
-- decision by the panel or by a Judge member; (5): to an agreed decision under s 103(2); (6): to "a decision of a kind
-- prescribed by the rules". s 131W: no application for a decision on review of an eligible social services decision, or
-- on second review. s 124(2)(b): the application must explain why the decision raises an issue of significance or may
-- contain a material error of fact or law. JUST FALSE: an exception (the text's "does not apply", "cannot be made").
-- NOTHING: not a s 105 decision, or I was never a party (s 123 is silent).
@export Can I apply to have the Administrative Review Tribunal's decision on my review referred to its guidance and appeals panel (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 123, 124, 131W)? A person who was at any time a party may apply to the President, explaining why the decision raises an issue of significance or may contain a material error of fact or law. Not for a decision made by the panel or a Judge, an agreed decision, a kind of decision the rules exclude, or social services decisions (which go to second review instead).
GIVEN `a decision on the review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is the Tribunal's decision on the review (affirming, varying or setting aside the original decision)
      `I was a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were at any time a party to the proceeding
      `made by the panel or a Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made by the guidance and appeals panel, or by a Tribunal member who is a Judge (or a Tribunal including one)
      `agreed by the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made in the terms the parties agreed (s 103(2))
      `a kind the rules exclude` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is of a kind the Tribunal's rules prescribe as not able to be referred
      `a social services review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was on review of a social security, family assistance, child support, paid parental leave or student assistance decision, or on second review
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the guidance and appeals panel question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `a decision on the review`) OR (NOT `I was a party`)
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `made by the panel or a Judge` OR `agreed by the parties` OR `a kind the rules exclude` OR `a social services review`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 125: "within the period of 28 days after the Tribunal gives a statement of reasons for the decision to the parties ...
-- (or such longer period as the President, in special circumstances, allows)".
@export By when must I apply to have the Administrative Review Tribunal's decision referred to the guidance and appeals panel (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 125)? Within 28 days after the Tribunal gives the parties its statement of reasons, unless the President allows longer in special circumstances.
GIVEN `reasons given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal gave the parties its statement of reasons for the decision
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to the guidance and appeals panel question` `reasons given on` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `reasons given on`

§§ `Second review of a social services decision (Part 5A) [if my review was about a social services decision]`

-- s 131C: the eligible social services decisions. s 131D(1): "A person whose interests are affected by an ART social
-- services decision may apply ... for review (the second review)". (2): "cannot be made" for a decision agreed under s
-- 103(2). s 131H: in a Paid Parental Leave Act 2010 claimant decision, not "in the person's capacity as an employer".
-- JUST FALSE: (2), s 131H, not affected (the text's no is (2) and 131H; not affected is fork F3). NOTHING: not an eligible
-- social services decision (Part 5A does not reach it).
DECLARE `My first decision was under` IS ONE OF
    `the Social Security Act 1991 or the Social Security (Administration) Act 1999`
    `the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Act 1999`
    `the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) (Administration) Act 1999, not a child care provider decision`
    `the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) (Administration) Act 1999, a child care provider decision`
    `the Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988: a care percentage decision, a s 92 refusal of an extension, or a s 95N(2) determination`
    `the Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988: another decision`
    `the Paid Parental Leave Act 2010: a claimant decision`
    `the Paid Parental Leave Act 2010: another decision`
    `the Student Assistance Act 1973`
    `another Act`

GIVEN k IS A `My first decision was under`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 131C -- an eligible social services decision` k IF
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `the Social Security Act 1991 or the Social Security (Administration) Act 1999` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Act 1999` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) (Administration) Act 1999, not a child care provider decision` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988: a care percentage decision, a s 92 refusal of an extension, or a s 95N(2) determination` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Paid Parental Leave Act 2010: a claimant decision` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Student Assistance Act 1973` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export The Administrative Review Tribunal has decided my review of a social security, family assistance, child support, paid parental leave or student assistance decision: can I apply for a second review (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 131C, 131D, 131H)? A person whose interests are affected may apply for second review, unless the Tribunal's decision was made in terms the parties agreed. In a paid parental leave claimant decision, an employer cannot apply as employer.
GIVEN `first decision under` IS A `My first decision was under` @desc The law and kind of the original decision that the Tribunal reviewed (or made)
      `my interests are affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's decision affects your interests
      `agreed by the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's decision was made in the terms the parties agreed (s 103(2))
      `applying as an employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be applying in your capacity as an employer
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for second review question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`s 131C -- an eligible social services decision` `first decision under`)
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `agreed by the parties`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `applying as an employer` AND `first decision under` EQUALS `the Paid Parental Leave Act 2010: a claimant decision`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `my interests are affected`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 131J(1): second review "must be made within the time prescribed under section 18". (2): for a child care decision
-- about "an individual's entitlement to be paid CCS for a week", also later if "(a) because of a review, by the
-- Commissioner of Taxation, of a previous decision ... about the taxable income ...; and (b) within the time prescribed
-- under that section after the individual or other person was notified by the Commissioner of the outcome of the review."
-- The prescribed days are an input. JUST TRUE: in time under (2). JUST FALSE: (2) does not help (its "if" fails).
@export My second review is about child care subsidy for a week, and the Tax Commissioner has reviewed my (or my partner's) taxable income: can I apply for second review after the usual time (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 131J(2))? Yes, if you apply because of that review and within the usual prescribed time counted from when the Commissioner notified the outcome.
GIVEN `a child care subsidy decision for a week` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is a child care decision about an individual's entitlement to be paid child care subsidy for a week
      `because of a tax review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are applying because the Commissioner of Taxation reviewed an earlier decision about your (or the relevant other person's) taxable income for that income year
      `notified of the outcome on` IS A DATE @desc The day you (or the other person) were notified by the Commissioner of the outcome of the review
      `days prescribed` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the Tribunal's rules prescribe for applying (s 18)
      `applying on` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or would apply)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for second review late after a tax review question` MEANS
    IF `a child care subsidy decision for a week` AND `because of a tax review` AND `s 18 -- an application on this day is in time` `days prescribed` (`the days from` `notified of the outcome on` `to` `applying on`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Appeal to the Federal Court (Part 7 Division 2) [if I think the Tribunal got the law wrong]`

-- s 172(1): "A party to a proceeding in the Tribunal may appeal to the Federal Court, on a question of law, from the
-- decision of the Tribunal". (2): not a President's decision under s 128 (whether to refer to the panel). s 173: "A person
-- may appeal ... from a decision of the Tribunal that the person's interests are not affected by a reviewable decision."
-- JUST TRUE: I may appeal. JUST FALSE: a s 128 decision (the text's "does not apply"). NOTHING: no question of law, or I
-- was not a party and it is not a standing decision (fork F4).
@export Can I appeal to the Federal Court from the Administrative Review Tribunal's decision (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 172, 173)? A party may appeal on a question of law. A person the Tribunal decided is not affected by the decision may appeal that finding. The President's decision whether to refer a matter to the guidance and appeals panel cannot be appealed this way.
GIVEN `I was a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a party to the Tribunal proceeding
      `a standing decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision you want to appeal is the Tribunal's decision that your interests are not affected by the decision under review
      `a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your appeal is on a question of law (not just disagreement with the facts found)
      `the President's referral decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is the President's decision whether to refer a Tribunal decision to the guidance and appeals panel (s 128)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal to the Federal Court question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the President's referral decision`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `a standing decision`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `I was a party` AND `a question of law`
              THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 174(1): "(a) if ... the Tribunal is required to give a statement of reasons ... [or] the party ... has a right to
-- request a statement of reasons ... and exercises the right -- no later than 28 days after the Tribunal gives the party or
-- person its statement of reasons ...; (b) if the party ... has a right to request a statement of reasons ... and does not
-- exercise the right -- no later than 28 days after the last day on which the party or person could have requested a
-- statement of reasons ...; or (c) in any other case -- no later than 28 days after the party or person is given the
-- decision." (2): the Federal Court may extend. (2A): "do not take into account any day in the period starting on 24
-- December in a year and ending on 14 January in the next year." (3): a guidance and appeals panel application stops the
-- count from "the day the application is made" to "the day the President refers the decision or refuses the application".
-- s 131X: second review stops it from the day the application is made to the day of notice that the Tribunal is constituted
-- (or that I am not affected). The last day to request reasons under s 112(5) is 28 days after the decision is made.
DECLARE `My reasons position` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal must give reasons (a decision on the review, s 111)`
    `I could request reasons and did`
    `I could request reasons and did not`
    `neither (any other case)`

@export By when must I appeal to the Federal Court from the Administrative Review Tribunal's decision (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) ss 174, 131X)? Within 28 days after you are given the Tribunal's reasons (or after the last day you could have asked for them, or after you are given the decision, depending on the case). Days from 24 December to 14 January are not counted, nor the days while an application to the guidance and appeals panel or for second review is pending. The Federal Court may extend the time.
GIVEN `my position` IS A `My reasons position` @desc Whether the Tribunal had to give you reasons, or you could ask for them (and did or did not)
      `reasons given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal gave you its statement of reasons (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made its decision
      `decision given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given the decision
      `panel application made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied to refer the decision to the guidance and appeals panel
      `panel applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day that application was made (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `President decided on` IS A DATE @desc The day the President referred the decision or refused the application (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `second review applied for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied for second review of a social services decision
      `second review applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied for second review (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `second review notice on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice that the Tribunal was constituted for the second review, or that your interests are not affected (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I appeal to the Federal Court question` MEANS
    `the last of` 28 `counted days after` `the base day` `skipping` `panel application made` `panel applied on` `President decided on` `and` `second review applied for` `second review applied on` `second review notice on`
    WHERE
        `the base day` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my position`
            WHEN `the Tribunal must give reasons (a decision on the review, s 111)` THEN `reasons given on`
            WHEN `I could request reasons and did` THEN `reasons given on`
            WHEN `I could request reasons and did not` THEN `the date` 28 `days after` `decision made on`
            OTHERWISE `decision given on`

§§ `Help with the costs of a review (s 294)`

-- s 294(1): a person who "applies, or proposes to apply, to the Tribunal for review of a reviewable decision may apply to
-- the Attorney-General ... for assistance". (2): the same for a guidance and appeals panel application; (3): another
-- party; (4): a court proceeding "in respect of a matter arising under this Act". (5), (6): not for a kind of proceeding or
-- leave application "prescribed by the rules". (7): the Attorney-General may authorise legal or financial assistance if it
-- "would involve hardship to the person to refuse" and it is "reasonable" that it be granted. (9): nothing done under the
-- section "extends a period for making an application to the Tribunal".
DECLARE `My role for assistance` IS ONE OF
    `I apply or propose to apply for review (s 294(1))`
    `I apply or propose to apply to the guidance and appeals panel (s 294(2))`
    `I am or propose to become another party to a Tribunal proceeding (s 294(3))`
    `I bring or am a party to a court proceeding on a matter arising under this Act (s 294(4))`
    `none of these`

@export Can I ask the Attorney-General for legal or financial help with my Administrative Review Tribunal case, or a court case about it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 294(1)-(6), (9))? An applicant or intending applicant, a guidance and appeals panel applicant, another party, or a party to a court proceeding under the Act may apply, unless the rules exclude that kind of proceeding. Applying does not extend your time to apply to the Tribunal.
GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role for assistance` @desc Your role in the review or court proceeding
      `a kind the rules exclude` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's rules prescribe your kind of proceeding (or panel application) as one for which assistance cannot be sought
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the Attorney-General for assistance question` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my role` EQUALS `none of these`
              THEN NOTHING
           IF `a kind the rules exclude`
              THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export I have asked the Attorney-General for help with my Administrative Review Tribunal case: can the Attorney-General grant it (Administrative Review Tribunal Act 2024 (Cth) s 294(7), (8))? The Attorney-General may authorise legal or financial assistance, on conditions, if refusing would cause you hardship and granting is reasonable in all the circumstances.
GIVEN `refusal would cause hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General considers it would involve hardship to you to refuse
      `reasonable to grant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General considers it reasonable in all the circumstances to grant your application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Attorney-General grant me assistance question` MEANS
    IF `refusal would cause hardship` AND `reasonable to grant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
